    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d134">

                <pb facs="0382" n="96" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg96"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/215</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-12-18">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 18, 1916, noon</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received 2.40 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5335. Your circular telegram of December 16, 5 p. m. The text of the German note
                    was delivered personally by me at 11.30 to-day to Lord Robert Cecil in charge of
                    Foreign Office during temporary absence of Mr. Balfour. I handed him a copy of
                    it. I read to him the rest of your telegram but left no copy of that in the
                    absence of explicit instructions to do so. He informed me that before he could
                    give an intimation of the answer that will be made to the Central powers the
                    cabinet must be consulted, and he promised to give me the intimation you ask for
                    at the earliest possible moment. He did intimate that the answer would be a
                    single answer signed by all the Allied Governments, and not a separate reply by
                    each.</p>
                <p>It is generally expected here that the Prime Minister will indicate in his speech
                    to-morrow in the House of Commons, on the general policy of the new government,
                    the nature of the response that the Allied Governments will make to the Central
                    powers.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
